Sending keys: ['cat > /app/output/answer.txt <<\'EOF\'\n## Statute\n\n**§7703(b) Certain married individuals living apart** (lines 517-532):\n"For purposes of those provisions of this title which refer to this subsection, if-\n    (1) an individual who is married (within the meaning of subsection (a)) and who files a separate return maintains as his home a household which constitutes for more than one-half of the taxable year the principal place of abode of a child with respect to whom such individual is entitled to a deduction for the taxable year under section 151,\n    (2) such individual furnishes over one-half of the cost of maintaining such household during the taxable year, and\n    (3) during the last 6 months of the taxable year, such individual\'s spouse is not a member of such household,\nsuch individual shall not be considered as married."\n\n**§2(b)(1) Definition of head of household** (lines 204-225):\n"An individual shall be considered a head of a household if, and only if, such individual is not married at the close of his taxable year, is not a surviving spouse (as defined in subsection (a)), and either-\n    (A) maintains as his home a household which constitutes for more than one-half of such taxable year the principal place of abode, as a member of such household, of-\n        (i) a qualifying child of the individual (as defined in section 152(c)), but not if such child-\n            (I) is married at the close of the taxpayer\'s taxable year, and\n            (II) is not a dependent of such individual by reason of section 152(b)(2) or\n..."\n\n**§63(c)(2)(B) Basic standard deduction** (lines 386-394):\n"For purposes of paragraph (1), the basic standard deduction is-\n    (A) 200 percent of the dollar amount in effect under subparagraph (C) for the taxable year in the case of-\n        (i) a joint return, or\n        (ii) a surviving spouse (as defined in section 2(a)),\n    (B) $4,400 in the case of a head of household (as defined in section 2(b)), or\n    (C) $3,000 in any other case."\n\n**§151(d)(1) Exemption amount** (lines 133-135):\n"For purposes of this section-\n    (1) In general\n    Except as otherwise provided in this subsection, the term \'exemption amount\' means $2,000."\n\n**§1(b)(ii) Tax for heads of households** (lines 20-22):\n"There is hereby imposed on the taxable income of every head of a household (as defined in section 2(b)) a tax determined in accordance with the following:\n    (i) 15% of taxable income if the taxable income is not over $29,600;\n    (ii) $4,440, plus 28% of the excess over $29,600 if the taxable income is over $29,600 but not over $76,400;"\n\n## Reasoning\n\n1. **Filing Status Determination (§7703(b))**: Alice is married but files a separate return. She maintains a household for her son Charlie (who was born September 16th, 2017, making him a qualifying child) for more than one-half of 2019 (since they\'ve lived together since September 16th, 2017). Alice furnishes over one-half the cost of maintaining the household (she maintains the home). Bob (her spouse) has a different principal place of abode than Alice and Charlie in 2019, meaning Bob is not a member of Alice\'s household for the last 6 months of 2019. Under §7703(b), Alice "shall not be considered as married."\n\n2. **Head of Household Status (§2(b)(1))**: Since Alice is not considered married at the close of her taxable year, maintains a household that constitutes for more than one-half of the taxable year the principal place of abode of her qualifying child Charlie, and is entitled to a deduction for Charlie under §151, Alice qualifies as a head of household.\n\n3. **Standard Deduction (§63(c)(2)(B))**: As a head of household, Alice\'s basic standard deduction is $4,400. Alice is not aged (born after 1954) or blind, so no additional standard deduction applies.\n\n4. **Personal Exemptions (§151)**: Alice is entitled to:\n   - One exemption for herself: $2,000 (§151(d)(1))\n   - One exemption for dependent Charlie: $2,000 (§151(c) and (d)(1))\n   Total exemptions: $4,000\n\n5. **Taxable Income Calculation**:\n   Gross income: $73,124 (given fact)\n   Standard deduction: $4,400 (§63(c)(2)(B))\n   Exemptions: $4,000 (§151)\n   Taxable income = $73,124 - $4,400 - $4,000 = $64,724\n\n6. **Tax Calculation (§1(b)(ii))**: Alice\'s taxable income of $64,724 is over $29,600 but not over $76,400.\n   Tax = $4,440 + 28% × ($64,724 - $29,600)\n   Tax = $4,440 + 0.28 × $35,124\n   Tax = $4,440 + $9,834.72\n   Tax = $14,274.72\n   Rounded to whole number: $14,275\n\n## Answer\nAnswer: 14275\nEOF\n'] min_timeout_sec: 0.5 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
