ch taxable year begins.\n(2) Relationship\n(H) An individual (other than an individual who at any time during the taxable year was the spouse, determined without regard to section 7703, of the taxpayer) who, for the taxable year of the taxpayer, has the same principal place of abode as the taxpayer and is a member of the taxpayer\'s household."\n\n**Section 151(d)(5) - Exemption amount for 2018-2025 (lines 97-98):**\n"(5) Special rules for taxable years 2018 through 2025\nIn the case of a taxable year beginning after December 31, 2017, and before January 1, 2026, the term "exemption amount" means zero."\n\n**Section 63(c)(2)(B) and (7) - Standard deduction (lines 393-422):**\n"(2) Basic standard deduction\n(B) $4,400 in the case of a head of household (as defined in section 2(b)), or\n(7) Special rules for taxable years 2018 through 2025\nIn the case of a taxable year beginning after December 31, 2017, and before January 1, 2026-\nParagraph (2) shall be applied-\n(i) by substituting "$12,000" for "$4,400" in subparagraph (B)"\n\n**Section 1(b) - Tax rates for heads of households (lines 21-29):**\n"(b) Heads of households\nThere is hereby imposed on the taxable income of every head of a household (as defined in section 2(b)) a tax determined in accordance with the following:\n(v) $77,485, plus 39.6% of the excess over $250,000 if the taxable income is over $250,000."\n\n## Reasoning\n\n**Step 1: Determine Bob\'s filing status for 2018**\n\nAlice died on July 9, 2014. For the 2018 tax year, Alice\'s death was 4 years prior, which exceeds the "two years immediately preceding the taxable year" requirement in section 2(a)(1)(A). Therefore, Bob does not qualify as a surviving spouse.\n\nBob is unmarried at the close of 2018 (spouse died in 2014).\n\n**Step 2: Determine if Bob qualifies as head of household**\n\nBob furnished the costs of maintaining the home where he and Charlie lived during 2015-2019. For 2018, Bob furnished over half the cost of maintaining the household.\n\nCharlie has no income and is not the qualifying child of any taxpayer. Under section 152(d), Charlie qualifies as a qualifying relative because:\n- Charlie has no income (section 152(d)(1)(B))\n- Charlie is not a qualifying child of any taxpayer (given in facts)\n- Charlie has the same principal place of abode as Bob and is a member of Bob\'s household (section 152(d)(2)(H))\n\nTherefore, Charlie is a dependent of Bob under section 151(c).\n\nUnder section 2(b)(1)(A)(ii), Bob qualifies as head of household because he maintains a household that is the principal place of abode of Charlie, who is a dependent of Bob.\n\n**Step 3: Calculate taxable income**\n\nBob\'s AGI: $304,598 (given in facts)\n\nStandard deduction for head of household in 2018: $12,000 (section 63(c)(7)(i))\n\nExemption amount for 2018: $0 (section 151(d)(5))\n\nTaxable income = AGI - Standard deduction = $304,598 - $12,000 = $292,598\n\n**Step 4: Calculate tax**\n\nTaxable income of $292,598 is over $250,000, so under section 1(b)(v):\nTax = $77,485 + 39.6% × ($292,598 - $250,000)\n\nComputation:\nEOF\n'] min_timeout_sec: 0.5 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
