Sending keys: ['cat > /app/output/answer.txt << \'EOF\'\n## Statute\n\nSection 3306(a)(2)(B) (§3306. Definitions, line 265-271):\n\n"(2) Agricultural labor\n\nIn the case of agricultural labor, the term "employer" means, with respect to any calendar year, any person who-\n\n    (A) during the calendar year or the preceding calendar year paid wages of $20,000 or more for agricultural labor, or\n\n    (B) on each of some 10 days during the calendar year or during the preceding calendar year, each day being in a different calendar week, employed at least 5 individuals in employment in agricultural labor for some portion of the day."\n\n## Reasoning\n\nSection 3306(a)(2)(B) defines when a person is an "employer" for agricultural labor purposes. The condition requires that the person employed at least 5 individuals in agricultural labor on each of some 10 days during the calendar year, with each day being in a different calendar week.\n\nApplying the facts to this provision:\n\n1. Dates in 2017 when Alice employed at least 5 agricultural workers:\n   - Jan 24: 5 workers (Bob, Cameron, Dan, Emily, Fred)\n   - Mar 3: 5 workers (Bob, Cameron, Dan, Emily, Fred)\n   - Mar 18: 5 workers (Cameron, Dan, Emily, Fred, George)\n   - Apr 1: 5 workers (Bob, Cameron, Dan, Fred, George)\n   - May 9: 5 workers (Cameron, Dan, Emily, Fred, George)\n   - Oct 14: 5 workers (Bob, Cameron, Dan, Emily, George)\n   - Nov 8: 5 workers (Bob, Cameron, Emily, Fred, George)\n   - Nov 22: 5 workers (Bob, Cameron, Dan, Emily, Fred)\n   - Dec 1: 5 workers (Bob, Cameron, Dan, Emily, George)\n   - Dec 2: 5 workers (Bob, Cameron, Dan, Emily, George)\n\n2. Computation of unique calendar weeks (using ISO week numbers):\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\ndates_with_5plus = [\n    date(2017, 1, 24), date(2017, 3, 3), date(2017, 3, 18),\n    date(2017, 4, 1), date(2017, 5, 9), date(2017, 10, 14),\n    date(2017, 11, 8), date(2017, 11, 22), date(2017, 12, 1),\n    date(2017, 12, 2)\n]\nweeks = set(d.isocalendar()[1] for d in dates_with_5plus)\nprint(len(weeks))  # Output: 9\n```\n\n3. Result: There are 10 dates with 5+ workers, but they fall in only 9 different calendar weeks (Dec 1 and Dec 2 are in the same calendar week, week 48 of 2017).\n\n4. Since Section 3306(a)(2)(B) requires 10 different calendar weeks and Alice only has 9, the condition is NOT met.\n\nTherefore, Section 3306(a)(2)(B) does NOT apply to Alice for the year 2017.\n\n## Answer\nAnswer: Contradiction\nEOF\n'] min_timeout_sec: 0.5 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
