so 12 years old) (§152(c)(3)). Charlie qualifies as a qualifying child under §152(c).\n\n**Step 3: Determine deductions**\nAlice\'s gross income for 2017 was $567,192. She donated $59,850 to charity.\n\nStandard deduction for surviving spouse: 200% × $3,000 = $6,000 (§162(c)(2)(A), §162(c)(2)(C)).\n\nSince itemized deduction ($59,850) > standard deduction ($6,000), Alice itemizes.\n\n**Step 4: Apply §68 reduction**\n§68(f) states §68 does NOT apply to years beginning AFTER December 31, 2017. Since 2017 begins on January 1, 2017 (not after December 31, 2017), §68 DOES apply.\n\nUnder §68(a), itemized deductions are reduced by the lesser of:\n- 3% of AGI excess over applicable amount (§68(b)(1)(A) = $300,000 for surviving spouse)\n- 80% of otherwise allowable deductions\n\nAGI before §68 reduction = $567,192 - $59,850 = $507,342\nExcess over $300,000 = $507,342 - $300,000 = $207,342\n3% of excess = 0.03 × $207,342 = $6,220.26\n80% of deductions = 0.80 × $59,850 = $47,880\n\nReduction = min($6,220.26, $47,880) = $6,220.26\nAllowed itemized deduction = $59,850 - $6,220.26 = $53,629.74\n\nAGI = $567,192 - $53,629.74 = $513,562.26\n\n**Step 5: Personal exemption phaseout**\nExemption amount = $2,000 (§151(d)(1)). This is NOT zero because §151(d)(5) only applies to years beginning AFTER December 31, 2017.\n\nNumber of exemptions: Alice + Charlie = 2 (§151(b), §151(c))\n\nPhaseout (§151(d)(3)(B)): AGI ($513,562.26) exceeds applicable amount ($300,000) by $213,562.26.\nFraction of $2,500 = $213,562.26 / $2,500 = 85.424904\nRounded up to 86 fractions ("or fraction thereof")\nPhaseout percentage = 86 × 2% = 172%, capped at 100% (§151(d)(3)(B))\n\nSince phaseout is 100%, total exemptions = $0.\n\n**Step 6: Taxable income**\nTaxable income = AGI - exemptions = $513,562.26 - $0 = $513,562.26\n\n**Step 7: Calculate tax**\nAlice\'s taxable income ($513,562.26) exceeds $250,000, so §1(a)(1)(v) applies:\nTax = $75,528.50 + 39.6% × ($513,562.26 - $250,000)\nTax = $75,528.50 + 0.396 × 