0 in the case of a head of household (as defined in section 2(b)), or"\n\n## Reasoning\n\n**Step 1: Determine Alice\'s filing status**\n\n- Alice\'s spouse Bob died on July 9, 2014 (case fact)\n- For the 2017 taxable year, the two years immediately preceding are 2015 and 2016\n- Under §2(a)(1)(A), a surviving spouse\'s spouse must have died "during either of the two years immediately preceding the taxable year"\n- Since Bob died in 2014 (not 2015 or 2016), Alice does NOT qualify as a surviving spouse for 2017\n\n**Step 2: Check if Alice qualifies as head of household**\n\n- Alice is not married at the close of her 2017 taxable year (Bob died in 2014)\n- Alice is not a surviving spouse (as established above)\n- Alice furnished over half the cost of maintaining the home where Charlie lived from 2004 to 2019 (case fact: "From 2004 to 2019, Alice furnished the costs of maintaining the home")\n- Charlie is Alice\'s child (born October 9, 2000), so Charlie is a qualifying child under §152(c)\n- Under §2(b)(1), Alice qualifies as head of household\n\n**Step 3: Calculate deductions**\n\n- Standard deduction for head of household: $4,400 (§63(c)(2)(B))\n- Personal exemptions:\n  - Alice\'s own exemption: $2,000 (§151(d)(1))\n  - Charlie\'s exemption as dependent: $2,000 (§151(c))\n  - Total exemptions: $2,000 + $2,000 = $4,000\n\n**Step 4: Calculate taxable income**\n\n- Gross income: $25,561 (case fact)\n- Taxable income = Gross income - Standard deduction - Personal exemptions\n- Taxable income = $25,561 - $4,400 - $4,000 = $17,161\n\n**Step 5: Calculate tax**\n\n- Taxable income of $17,161 is not over $29,600\n- Under §1(b)(i), tax is 15% of taxable income\n- Tax = 15% × $17,161 = $2,574.15\n- Rounding to whole dollar: $2,574\n\n## Answer\nAnswer: 2574\nEOF\n'] min_timeout_sec: 0.5 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
