Sending keys: ['cat > /app/output/answer.txt <<\'EOF\'\n## Statute\n\n**§1(d) Married individuals filing separate returns (lines 39-48):**\n"There is hereby imposed on the taxable income of every married individual (as defined in section 7703) who does not make a single return jointly with his spouse, a tax determined in accordance with the following:\n    (i) 15% of taxable income if the taxable income is not over $18,450;\n    (ii) $2,767.50, plus 28% of the excess over $18,450 if the taxable income is over $18,450 but not over $44,575;"\n\n**§151(d)(1) Exemption amount (line 72):**\n"Except as otherwise provided in this subsection, the term \'exemption amount\' means $2,000."\n\n**§63(c)(2) Basic standard deduction (lines 384-397):**\n"For purposes of paragraph (1), the basic standard deduction is-\n        (A) 200 percent of the dollar amount in effect under subparagraph (C) for the taxable year in the case of-\n            (i) a joint return, or\n            (ii) a surviving spouse (as defined in section 2(a)),\n        (B) $4,400 in the case of a head of household (as defined in section 2(b)), or\n        (C) $3,000 in any other case."\n\n**§63(f)(1) Additional amounts for the aged (lines 442-450):**\n"The taxpayer shall be entitled to an additional amount of $600-\n        (A) for himself if he has attained age 65 before the close of his taxable year, and\n        (B) for the spouse of the taxpayer if the spouse has attained age 65 before the close of the taxable year and an additional exemption is allowable to the taxpayer for such spouse under section 151(b)."\n\n## Reasoning\n\n**Case Facts:**\n- Alice\'s income in 2017: $36,266\n- Alice and Bob married since Feb 3rd, 2017 (both alive in 2017)\n- Alice born March 2nd, 1950; Bob born March 3rd, 1951\n- Filing status: Married filing separately (Alice takes standard deduction)\n- Alice\'s age in 2017: 2017 - 1950 = 67 (attained age 65 before close of taxable year)\n- Bob\'s age in 2017: 2017 - 1951 = 66\n- Bob has no gross income in 2017\n\n**Step 1: Filing Status**\nAlice and Bob are married in 2017 (married since Feb 3rd, 2017). They file separately, so Alice is subject to §1(d) - Married individuals filing separate returns.\n\n**Step 2: Exemptions (§151(b) and §151(d)(1))**\n- Exemption amount = $2,000 (line 72)\n- Taxpayer exemption = $2,000\n- Spouse exemption = $2,000 (Bob has no gross income per §151(b))\n- Total exemptions = $2,000 + $2,000 = $4,000\n\n**Step 3: Standard Deduction (§63)**\n- Basic standard deduction for "any other case" (married filing separately) = $3,000 (§63(c)(2)(C), lines 384-397)\n- Additional standard deduction for taxpayer aged (§63(f)(1)(A)) = $600 (Alice is 67, over 65)\n- Additional standard deduction for spouse aged (§63(f)(1)(B)) = $600 (Bob is 66, over 65, and §151(b) exemption is allowable since Bob has no income)\n- Total standard deduction = $3,000 + $600 + $600 = $4,200\n\n**Step 4: Taxable Income**\nTaxable income = Gross income - Exemptions - Standard deduction\nTaxable income = $36,266 - $4,000 - $4,200 = $28,066\n\n**Step 5: Tax Calculation (§1(d))**\nTaxable income of $28,066 falls in bracket (ii): over $18,450 but not over $44,575\nTax = $2,767.50 + 28% of excess over $18,450\nTax = $2,767.50 + 0.28 × ($28,066 - $18,450)\nTax = $2,767.50 + 0.28 × $9,616\nTax = $2,767.50 + $2,692.48\nTax = $5,459.98\n\nRounded to whole number: $5,460\n\n## Answer\nAnswer: 5460\nEOF\n'] min_timeout_sec: 0.5 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
