andard deduction** (lines 372-388)\n\n(c) Standard deduction\n\nFor purposes of this subtitle-\n\n    (2) Basic standard deduction\n\n    For purposes of paragraph (1), the basic standard deduction is-\n\n        (A) 200 percent of the dollar amount in effect under subparagraph (C) for the taxable year in the case of-\n\n            (i) a joint return, or\n\n            (ii) a surviving spouse (as defined in section 2(a)),\n\n        (B) $4,400 in the case of a head of household (as defined in section 2(b)), or\n\n        (C) $3,000 in any other case.\n\n**§68. Overall limitation on itemized deductions** (lines 475-494)\n\n(b) Applicable amount\n\n    (1) In general\n\n    For purposes of this section, the term "applicable amount" means-\n\n        (A) $300,000 in the case of a joint return or a surviving spouse (as defined in section 2(a)),\n\n## Reasoning\n\n**Step 1: Determine filing status**\nAlice files a joint return with her spouse. Per §1(a), the tax brackets for "Married individuals filing joint returns" apply.\n\n**Step 2: Calculate standard deduction**\nPer §152(c)(2)(A), the basic standard deduction for a joint return is 200% of the amount in §152(c)(2)(C), which is $3,000.\nStandard deduction = 200% × $3,000 = $6,000\n\n**Step 3: Calculate exemption phaseout**\nPer §151(d)(1), the exemption amount is $2,000 per exemption.\nAlice and spouse each get one exemption: 2 × $2,000 = $4,000\n\nPer §151(d)(3)(A) and §68(b)(A), the applicable amount for joint returns is $300,000.\nAlice\'s gross income is $684,642.\n\nExcess AGI = $684,642 - $300,000 = $384,642\n\nPer §151(d)(3)(B), the applicable percentage is 2 percentage points for each $2,500 (or fraction thereof).\nNumber of $2,500 fractions = ceiling($384,642 / $2,500) = ceiling(153.8568) = 154\nApplicable percentage = 154 × 2% = 308%\n\nPer §151(d)(3)(B), the applicable percentage cannot exceed 100%.\nTherefore, exemption amount = $4,000 × 100% = $0 (fully phased out)\n\n**Step 4: Calculate taxable income*