Sending keys: ['cat > /app/output/answer.txt <<\'EOF\'\n## Statute\n\nSection 3306(c)(2) (lines 327-328):\n"(2) domestic service in a private home, local college club, or local chapter of a college fraternity or sorority unless performed for a person who paid cash remuneration of $1,000 or more to individuals employed in such domestic service in the calendar year or the preceding calendar year;"\n\nSection 3306(a)(3) (lines 273-274):\n"(3) Domestic service\n    In the case of domestic service in a private home, local college club, or local chapter of a college fraternity or sorority, the term \'employer\' means, with respect to any calendar year, any person who during the calendar year or the preceding calendar year paid wages in cash of $1,000 or more for such service."\n\n## Reasoning\n\n1. From the case facts: Alice paid wages of $3200 to Bob for domestic service done in her home from Feb 1st, 2017 to Sep 2nd, 2017.\n\n2. From Section 3306(c)(2): Domestic service in a private home is NOT considered "employment" UNLESS the person paid cash remuneration of $1,000 or more to individuals employed in such domestic service in the calendar year or preceding calendar year.\n\n3. From Section 3306(a)(3): For domestic service in a private home, the term "employer" means any person who during the calendar year or preceding calendar year paid wages in cash of $1,000 or more for such service.\n\n4. Computation (run with python3): python3 -c \'print(3200 > 1000, 3200 - 1000)\'\n   Result: True 2200\n   Since 3200 > 1000 is True, the exception in 3306(c)(2) does not apply, meaning the domestic service IS considered "employment" under the statute.\n\n5. Since Alice paid $3200 (which exceeds the $1000 threshold) for domestic service in her home in 2017, Section 3306(c)(2) applies to determine that this is employment (the exception for domestic service is overridden because the payment threshold was met).\n\n## Answer\nAnswer: Entailment\nEOF\n'] min_timeout_sec: 0.5 max_timeout_s