f a head of household (as defined in section 2(b))"\n\nSection 151(c) (line 64): "An exemption of the exemption amount for each individual who is a dependent (as defined in section 152) of the taxpayer for the taxable year."\n\nSection 151(d)(1) (line 70): "the term \'exemption amount\' means $2,000."\n\nSection 1(b)(i) (line 23): "15% of taxable income if the taxable income is not over $29,600"\n\n## Reasoning\n\n1. Filing Status: Alice was legally separated under a decree of divorce on March 2nd, 2015 (§7703(a)(2), line 510). Therefore, she is "not married" for tax purposes in 2017.\n\n2. Head of Household: Alice qualifies as head of household under §2(b)(1), line 204 because:\n   - She is not married (divorced in 2015)\n   - Charlie is her qualifying child\n   - Charlie is her child (§152(c)(2)(A), line 119)\n   - Charlie has the same principal place of abode as Alice for more than half the year ("Alice and Charlie live in a house maintained by Alice")\n   - Charlie is 5 years old in 2017 (born Feb 2, 2012), meeting the age requirement of <25 years (§152(c)(3), line 127)\n   - Charlie has not filed a joint return\n\n3. Standard Deduction: As a head of household, Alice\'s basic standard deduction is $4,400 (§63(c)(2)(B), line 394).\n\n4. Personal Exemptions: Alice gets:\n   - $2,000 for herself (§151(d)(1), line 70)\n   - $2,000 for Charlie as a dependent (§151(c), line 64; §151(d)(1), line 70)\n   - Total exemptions: $4,000\n\n5. Taxable Income Computation:\n   - Gross income: $9,560 (given fact)\n   - Less standard deduction: $4,400 (§63(c)(2)(B), line 394)\n   - Less exemptions: $4,000 (§151(c), line 64; §151(d)(1), line 70)\n   - Taxable income = $9,560 - $4,400 - $4,000 = $1,160\n\n6. Tax Calculation:\n   - Taxable income of $1,160 is not over $29,600\n   - Tax rate: 15% (§1(b)(i), line 23)\n   - Tax = 15% × $1,160 = $174\n\n## Answer\nAnswer: 174\nEOF\n'] min_timeout_sec: 0.5 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
