this paragraph if the individual is any of the following with respect to the taxpayer:\n\n        (E) A son or daughter of a brother or sister of the taxpayer.\n\n§151. Standard deduction\n\nLines 393-397:\n\n        (B) $4,400 in the case of a head of household (as defined in section 2(b)), or\n\n        (C) $3,000 in any other case.\n\n§151(c)(7) Special rules for taxable years 2018 through 2025\n\nLines 426-431:\n\n    (7) Special rules for taxable years 2018 through 2025\n\n    In the case of a taxable year beginning after December 31, 2017, and before January 1, 2026-\n\n    Paragraph (2) shall be applied-\n\n        (i) by substituting "$18,000" for "$4,400" in subparagraph (B), and\n\n        (ii) by substituting "$12,000" for "$3,000" in subparagraph (C).\n\n§2. Definitions and special rules\n\n(b) Definition of head of household\n\nLines 204-216:\n\n    (1) In general\n\n    An individual shall be considered a head of a household if, and only if, such individual is not married at the close of his taxable year, is not a surviving spouse (as defined in subsection (a)), and either-\n\n        (A) maintains as his home a household which constitutes for more than one-half of such taxable year the principal place of abode, as a member of such household, of-\n\n            (ii) any other person who is a dependent of the taxpayer, if the taxpayer is entitled to a deduction for the taxable year for such person under section 151,\n\n## Reasoning\n\n1. **Filing Status**: Alice is unmarried and maintains a household where Bob (her nephew) lived. Under §2(b)(1) (lines 204-216), Alice qualifies as "head of household" because she is not married, not a surviving spouse, and maintains a household where her dependent Bob lives.\n\n2. **Bob as Dependent**: Bob qualifies as a "qualifying relative" under §152(d) (lines 144-157) because:\n   - He bears a relationship to Alice as her brother\'s son (§152(d)(2)(E), lines 158-167)\n   - He has no income (§152(d)(1)(B), line 155)\n 