l rules for taxable years 2018 through 2025\n\nIn the case of a taxable year beginning after December 31, 2017, and before January 1, 2026, the term "exemption amount" means zero."\n\n§2. Surviving spouse and head of household defined (lines 189-240)\n\n"(b) Definition of head of household\n\n    (1) In general\n\n    An individual shall be considered a head of a household if, and only if, such individual is not married at the close of his taxable year, is not a surviving spouse (as defined in subsection (a)), and either-\n\n        (A) maintains as his home a household which constitutes for more than one-half of such taxable year the principal place of abode, as a member of such household, of-\n\n            (i) a qualifying child of the individual (as defined in section 152(c)), but not if such child-\n\n                (I) is married at the close of the taxpayer\'s taxable year, and\n\n                (II) is not a dependent of such individual by reason of section 152(b)(2)"\n\n§152. Dependent defined (lines 95-152)\n\n"(c) Qualifying child\n\nFor purposes of this section-\n\n    (1) In general\n\n    The term "qualifying child" means, with respect to any taxpayer for any taxable year, an individual-\n\n        (A) who bears a relationship to the taxpayer described in paragraph (2),\n\n        (B) who has the same principal place of abode as the taxpayer for more than one-half of such taxable year,\n\n        (C) who meets the age requirements of paragraph (3), and\n\n        (E) who has not filed a joint return (other than only for a claim of refund) with the individual\'s spouse for the taxable year beginning in the calendar year in which the taxable year of the taxpayer begins."\n\n"(2) Relationship\n\nFor purposes of paragraph (1)(A), an individual bears a relationship to the taxpayer described in this paragraph if such individual is-\n\n        (A) a child of the taxpayer or a descendant of such a child, or"\n\n"(3) Age requirements\n\nFor purposes of paragraph (1)(C), an individual meets the requirements of this paragraph if such individual is younger than the taxpayer claiming such individual as a qualifying child and is less than 25 years old at the end of the taxable year."\n\n## Reasoning\n\n1. **Filing Status**: Alice is a head of household under §2(b). She is not married, maintains a household with her son Bob who has the same principal place of abode as her, and Bob is not married. Bob qualifies as a qualifying child under §152(c) because he bears a relationship to Alice as her child (§152(c)(2)(A)), has the same principal place of abode as Alice for more than one-half of the taxable year (§152(c)(1)(B)), and has not filed a joint return (§152(c)(1)(E)).\n\n2. **Gross Income**: $22,895 (given in case facts).\n\n3. **Standard Deduction**: Under §63(c)(2)(B), the basic standard deduction for a head of household is $4,400. The special rules in §63(c)(7) for taxable years 2018 through 2025 do not apply because Alice\'s taxable year is 2017, which begins before December 31, 2017.\n\n4. **Personal Exemptions**: Under §151(b), Alice is entitled to an exemption of the exemption amount for herself. Under §151(c), Alice is entitled to an exemption for Bob as her dependent. Under §151(d)(1), the exemption amount is $2,000. The special rule in §151(d)(5) that sets the exemption amount to zero applies only to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2017, so it does not apply to Alice\'s 2017 taxable year. Thus, total exemptions = $2,000 (Alice) + $2,000 (Bob) = $4,000.\n\n5. **Taxable Income**: Computed as gross income minus standard deduction minus personal exemptions:\n\n   ```\n   Taxable Income = $22,895 - $4,400 - $4,000 = $14,495\n   ```\n\n6. **Tax Calculation**: Under §1(b), the tax for a head of household is 15% of taxable income if the taxable income is not over $29,600. Since $14,495 is not over $29,600:\n\n   ```\n   Tax = $14,495 × 0.15 = $2,174.25\n   ```\n\n   Rounding to the nearest whole dollar gives $2,174.\n\n## Answer\nAnswer: 2174\nEOF\n'] min_timeout_sec: 0.5 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
