Sending keys: ['cat > /app/output/answer.txt <<\'EOF\'\n## Statute\n\n§1. Tax imposed (lines 1-17):\n\n(a) Married individuals filing joint returns and surviving spouses\n\nThere is hereby imposed on the taxable income of-\n\n    (1) every married individual (as defined in section 7703) who makes a single return jointly with his spouse, and\n\n    (2) every surviving spouse (as defined in section 2(a)),\n\na tax determined in accordance with the following:\n\n    (i) 15% of taxable income if the taxable income is not over $36,900;\n    (ii) $5,535, plus 28% of the excess over $36,900 if the taxable income is over $36,900 but not over $89,150;\n    (iii) $20,165, plus 31% of the excess over $89,150 if the taxable income is over $89,150 but not over $140,000;\n    (iv) $35,928.50, plus 36% of the excess over $140,000 if the taxable income is over $140,000 but not over $250,000;\n    (v) $75,528.50, plus 39.6% of the excess over $250,000 if the taxable income is over $250,000.\n\n§151. Allowance of deductions for personal exemptions, subsection (d)(1) (lines 68-72):\n\nFor purposes of this section-\n\n    (1) In general\n\n    Except as otherwise provided in this subsection, the term "exemption amount" means $2,000.\n\n§152. Dependent defined, subsection (a) (lines 100-104):\n\nFor purposes of this subtitle, the term "dependent" means-\n\n    (1) a qualifying child, or\n\n    (2) a qualifying relative.\n\n§153. Standard deduction, subsection (a)(2) (lines 386-396):\n\n    (2) Basic standard deduction\n\n    For purposes of paragraph (1), the basic standard deduction is-\n\n        (A) 200 percent of the dollar amount in effect under subparagraph (C) for the taxable year in the case of-\n\n            (i) a joint return, or\n\n            (ii) a surviving spouse (as defined in section 2(a)),\n\n        (B) $4,400 in the case of a head of household (as defined in section 2(b)), or\n\n        (C) $3,000 in any other case.\n\n## Reasoning\n\n1. Filing status: Alice files a joint return with her spouse (case fact: "Alice files a joint return with her spouse for 2015"). Under §1(a), this falls under "Married individuals filing joint returns and surviving spouses."\n\n2. Gross income: $42,876 (case fact: "Alice\'s and her spouse\'s gross income for the year 2015 is $42876").\n\n3. Standard deduction: Under §153(a)(2)(A), the basic standard deduction for a joint return is 200 percent of $3,000 (from §153(a)(2)(C)) = $6,000.\n\n4. Exemptions: Under §151(d)(1), the exemption amount is $2,000. Alice is entitled to:\n   - An exemption for herself (taxpayer)\n   - An exemption for her spouse (joint return filer)\n   - An exemption for Bob (her son, who has the same principal place of abode as her per §152(c)(1)(B))\n   - Charlie is NOT Alice\'s dependent because Charlie\'s principal place of abode is with Bob (his father), not Alice. Under §152(c)(1)(B), a qualifying child must have the same principal place of abode as the taxpayer.\n   \n   Total exemptions = 3 × $2,000 = $6,000.\n\n5. Taxable income computation (lines 360-362): Taxable income = Gross income - Standard deduction - Exemptions\n   = $42,876 - $6,000 - $6,000 = $30,876\n\n6. Tax calculation: Under §1(a)(i), since taxable income ($30,876) is not over $36,900, the tax is 15% of taxable income.\n   Tax = 15% × $30,876 = $4,631.40, rounded to whole number = $4,631.\n\n## Answer\nAnswer: 4631\nEOF\n'] min_timeout_sec: 0.5 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
